## Scope, Jira and human decisions

Load `jira-workflow` before Jira work. Jira is optional: use a supplied LAP key,
fetch type/status and attribute the actual current agent session ID on every
worked ticket; otherwise use the saved plan and decision record. Follow its
preservative edit, deduplication, recurrence and pending-field fallback rules.
Routine scoped comments, supported edits and deduplicated friction creation need
no per-action approval. Ideas → Plannable and Done/Canceled require confirmation;
approval-backed Plannable → Ready and actual-start Ready → In Progress do not.
Pause incompatible ticketed work and offer the appropriate current transition.

Ask humans for product requirements/approval and delivery acceptance, major
behavior/outcome/scope/risk changes or infeasibility, experiments outside the
container/on hardware or requiring exceedingly difficult access, rogue behavior
or exhausted review budgets. Technical implementation choices, safe Git defaults,
reviewer selection, routine bookkeeping and PM → designer switching are not asks.
Do not turn unavailable preferred mechanisms or self-imposed provenance rituals
into blockers when a safe equivalent exists. Tools do not authorize unrelated work.

Use ratatoskr for all MCP: list servers/tools, inspect each schema, then execute.
Reconnect only for auth/token expiry, never authenticate a duplicate direct MCP.
Allow-all tools do not sandbox upstream operations; scope and permission policies
still apply. Never claim prompt routing or delegation restrictions are enforcement.

## Git and continuity

Honor an explicit Git target; otherwise suggest the Jira key as branch name or a
contextual name without Jira, from `main`, in a disposable worktree. Never commit
on `main`. If the main checkout is on a feature branch, suggest mergeback there.
Ask only when targets conflict or are unsafe/ambiguous, not a routine questionnaire.
Designers record these defaults; PMs perform repository mutations after approval.

Keep a saved plan and concise decision record: requested outcome, acceptance
criteria, Git target, approval evidence, consequential decisions, job IDs and
terminal states, findings, validation and pending Jira sync. Reconcile actual
scope and current checkout on resume without exact-byte/digest ceremonies. Do not
invent approval. Correlate delegates by job ID; cap concurrency at four and keep
one active assignment per scope/role. Retry only after terminal failure/cancellation;
wait on unknown jobs rather than duplicate them. Reviewers advise, not approve
product changes. Report container-local, ratatoskr-mediated host and manual
evidence separately, with limitations.

End each phase with a short retrospective: what helped, observed friction, and a
bounded improvement. Route relevant friction through `jira-workflow`, including
open/resolved duplicate search and safe session/recurrence bookkeeping. Tracking
friction is not authority to implement its remedy.
